 ## check.sh
 
-### §check-fails-open — the check never blocks a commit because of its own failure
-- Since: 2026-09-11
-- Decision: `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`. Every internal error still ends in `exit 0`; the script only warns.
-- Forbidden: `set -e` or `set -o errexit`, and any exit status other than 0.
-- Reason: with `set -e`, a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file aborted the check and blocked every commit in
-  the repository for a day (2026-09-10). The check is advisory by design.
+### §check-fails-closed — a failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit
+- Since: 2026-10-22
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`. Any failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit. The size findings
+  themselves are still only warnings: when every command succeeds the script ends in `exit 0`, large files or not.
+- Forbidden: removing `-e`, and `cmd && action` as the last statement of a loop body or of the script (when `cmd`
+  is false the statement returns 1, which `set -e` + `pipefail` turn into an abort). Use `if cmd; then action; fi`.
+- Reason: maintainer decision (zeriong, 2026-10-22), overriding §check-fails-open. The deleted-file failure behind
+  that record is now handled by the `[ -f "$f" ] || continue` guard instead of by ignoring errors.
+- Supersedes: §check-fails-open (2026-09-11). It used `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`, ended every run in `exit 0`
+  and forbade `set -e`, because on 2026-09-10 a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file under `set -e` blocked
+  every commit in the repository for a day.
